{"reference":"Romans 15","book":"Romans","chapter":15,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 1–13 bring the preceding disagreement into focus: those called strong are to carry the weaknesses of those without strength and seek the neighbor’s good and upbuilding, following Messiah’s pattern rather than self-pleasing. The movement from reciprocal welcome to scriptural citations about Gentile praise broadens this concern into Jewish-Gentile belonging, ending with hope, joy, peace, and the Spirit’s power. “Strong” is therefore not a license for dominance or for requiring vulnerable people to adapt.","verse_refs":["Romans 15:1-3","Romans 15:4-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_cf3d7a3560cb489480ac67cb"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"A Careful Welcome","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The call to bear with others directs the community toward peace, upbuilding, welcome, and restraint. It does not authorize shaming or forcing someone to eat or abstain against conscience.","verse_refs":["Romans 15:1-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_9fdc4c2703e24ce5cc31b889"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"people_places","title":"Paul’s Travel and Mission","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul describes his Gentile work as a public service of Messiah Jesus, using an offering metaphor for Gentiles made acceptable through the Spirit. His aim to proclaim where Messiah had not already been named marks a stated geographic and relational boundary, not a universal claim to territory or a ban on collaboration. Rome is presented as a hoped-for stop on a supported journey toward Spain; the chapter records the plan, not its completion.","verse_refs":["Romans 15:14-16","Romans 15:17-21","Romans 15:22-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_b7db00fbe8115da6e88560b3"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The Jerusalem Contribution","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Paul’s western travel plan is preceded by a concrete service: Macedonia and Achaia have contributed for poor believers in Jerusalem. He calls their participation both something they were pleased to do and a material obligation connected with Gentiles’ sharing in Jewish spiritual things. The language holds voluntary participation, obligation, and Jewish-Gentile solidarity together; it does not turn the contributors into debtors to a fundraiser or establish a universal church tax.","verse_refs":["Romans 15:25-27","Romans 15:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_2a0c23de37c8df406fd6613d","reader_path_romans_015_649c363db2b63ef10977a534"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Hope and Prayer amid Risk","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The prayer in verse 13 asks the God of hope to give joy and peace in believing so that hope may abound through the Spirit’s power; these are presented as divine gift rather than an emotional performance standard. At the chapter’s close, Paul also asks the Romans to strive with him in prayer for rescue from danger, acceptance of his Jerusalem service, and a joyful visit. Prayer here expresses solidarity amid concrete uncertainty, not a guarantee that removes the need for practical planning.","verse_refs":["Romans 15:13","Romans 15:30-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_a7fe5107a0cdbc5d7bdef5b8"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Argument Carried Forward","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Romans 15 continues the discussion of Romans 14, where eating, abstaining, wine, days, conscience, judgment, and accommodation are all in view. The text does not fully identify the groups called weak and strong, so equating weak with Jews or strong with Gentiles goes beyond what the letter establishes.","verse_refs":["Romans 14:1-23","Romans 16:1-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_120f751e4ad40a88a0536f77"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"Romans survives in several early papyrus witnesses—P10, P26, P27, P31, P40, P46, and P61—which preserve different portions of the letter. P46 is by far the most extensive early papyrus witness to Romans.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_015_5dcebe26c53532f8343119a2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}